.Dd August 13, 2026
.Dt YPSTNS.CONF 5
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm ypstns.conf
.Nd configuration file for ypstns
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
holds the settings of
.Xr ypstns 8
itself.
Everything about the STNS API - where it is, how to authenticate to it, how
long to wait for it - is in
.Xr stns.conf 5
instead, which is read by
.Xr stns-key-wrapper 8
as well and can be copied unchanged from any other machine running an STNS
client.
.Pp
The file must not be group writable or readable by anybody but its owner;
.Xr ypstns 8
refuses to read one that is.
It names no secret of its own, but every other daemon on the system makes this
check and an administrator who adds one later should not have to discover that
this file was the exception.
.Sh SYNTAX
The syntax is the one shared by the daemons in the base system.
.Pp
A
.Sq #
begins a comment and runs to the end of the line.
A backslash at the end of a line continues it.
An argument containing whitespace must be quoted.
.Pp
Macros are set as
.Ic name Ns = Ns Ar value
and expanded as
.Ic $name :
.Bd -literal -offset indent
dom = "stns"
domain $dom
.Ed
.Pp
.Ic include Ar file
reads another file at that point.
.Sh SETTINGS
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Ic domain Ar name
The YP domain to serve.
Required, with no default.
.Pp
A client asks for a domain by name, so a server that guessed the name would
simply never be asked anything, and there is no useful guess to make.
It must match what
.Xr domainname 1
reports on the machines that will use it, which
.Xr rc.conf.local 8
sets from
.Pa /etc/defaultdomain .
It has nothing to do with DNS and need not resemble a hostname.
.It Ic interval Ar seconds
How often to re-read the directory from the API.
Between 10 and 86400; the default is 60.
.Pp
The fetch happens in a separate process and never blocks a lookup, so this
trades freshness against load on the API server and nothing else.
.Dq rcctl reload ypstns
asks for one immediately.
.It Ic user Ar name
The account the fetcher runs as.
The default is
.Sy _ypstns .
.Pp
It is the process that holds a connection to the API and nothing else.
The half of the daemon that answers the RPC stays root; see the
.Sx SECURITY
section of
.Xr ypstns 8
for why it has to.
.It Ic allow Ar address Ns Op / Ns Ar prefix
.It Ic allow Ic any
Who may ask.
.Pp
By default the answer is this machine and nothing else.
.Xr ypstns 8
is nearly always run beside the
.Xr ypbind 8
that consumes it, and a YP server reachable from the network hands the whole
directory to anybody who can guess the domain name.
.Pp
.Ic allow
may be given more than once, and each one adds an IPv4 address or network to
the loopback that is always permitted.
.Ic allow any
turns the check off altogether and cannot be combined with a specific network -
saying both is a contradiction, and it is reported rather than resolved by
whichever came last.
.Pp
Only IPv4.
The YP protocol is Sun RPC over IPv4 and libc's YP client will not ask over
anything else, so an IPv6 entry could never match and would be a promise this
daemon cannot keep.
.El
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width /usr/local/share/examples/ypstns/ypstns.conf -compact
.It Pa /etc/ypstns.conf
The default location.
.It Pa /etc/stns/client/stns.conf
The STNS API client configuration.
.It Pa /usr/local/share/examples/ypstns/ypstns.conf
A commented sample.
.El
.Sh EXAMPLES
The whole file, for a machine serving only itself:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
domain "stns"
.Ed
.Pp
A server for a small network, checked every five minutes:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
domain   "example"
interval 300
allow    192.0.2.0/24
.Ed
.Pp
Check it before installing it:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ ypstns -n -f ./ypstns.conf
.Ed
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr stns.conf 5 ,
.Xr stns-key-wrapper 8 ,
.Xr ypstns 8
.Sh AUTHORS
.An zakinko Aq Mt zakinko@snowrabbit.org
